This is almost certainly an extremely naive question, but why should I use something like nginx for serving shiny apps (or not)?

I currently serve internal apps for my group using the open source version of shiny server. My only complaint at this point is the speed of my apps. Would using nginx or similar improve speed, security, and/or functionality? I know proxying will allow things like authentication and secure connections to be set up, but I'm primarily interested in speeding things up.

thanks,

-Brett

From u5ab11db47365dff1 at groups.google.com  Wed Apr  6 19:38:08 2016
From: u5ab11db47365dff1 at groups.google.com (Erik Westlund)
Date: Wed, 06 Apr 2016 19:38:08 +0000
Subject: Re: why use nginx?
Message-ID: <h0M7LvEMp3k.UuK7G96qAQAJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <h0M7LvEMp3k._-z7-ZiqAQAJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
References: <h0M7LvEMp3k._-z7-ZiqAQAJ@shiny-discuss.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/shiny-discuss/c/h0M7LvEMp3k/m/UuK7G96qAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

nginx will not speed up how fast a shiny app runs.

nginx in this case really just acts as a router, moving requests to a certain URI to your app.  after nginx hands off the request, its all up to shiny-server, and nginx cannot speed that up.

